# ======================================================================
# Analysis pipeline, steps 00-12 (cumulative; run top to bottom)
#   step00 : download of the openFDA cosmetic adverse event data
#   step01 : cleaning; reporting channel, reporting regime, product
#            category, event group and litigation-type flag; descriptives
#   step02 : signal detection methods (PRR, ROR, IC, EBGM, MH-ROR,
#            subgroup ROR, proposed regime- and burst-robust method RB),
#            application to the observed data, main Monte Carlo simulation
#   step03 : figures (1200 dpi PNG + PDF) and tables
#   step04 : robustness analyses - RB tuning constants, litigation spread
#            over several categories, alternative litigation-type
#            definitions in the observed data
#   step05 : report-level bootstrap, one event group per report,
#            overdispersed multi-event simulation, Hartung-Knapp pooling,
#            Benjamini-Hochberg FDR, classification validation sample
#   step06 : export of product-name strings and preferred terms with
#            counts, used to revise the keyword rules
#   step07 : keyword rules v3 and an independent validation sample
#   step08 : final keyword rules v4 and the final validation sample
#   step09 : probable duplicate reports (observed data); true signals in
#            the shocked category (simulation); non-litigation monthly
#            means over alternative reference periods
#   step10 : Paule-Mandel pooling and a trailing (prospective) baseline
#            window, simulation and observed data
#   step11 : report-level 2x2 tables (observed data and simulation),
#            serious-event enrichment in the mandatory regime, extreme
#            dominance of one category
#   step12 : true signal in the shocked pair itself; burst duration
#            (6, 9, 12, 18 months)
# Every step writes its outputs to the working folder and is skipped on
# later runs when these outputs already exist; nothing is re-downloaded.
# Before running, set the folder paths in `cand` (section 0) to a folder
# on your own computer.
# ======================================================================

